  data:  # 4ee8
    kind: work_item  # 4ee8
    title: "features[] is documented in spec as 'named slices' with full schema but always empty (schema.py:101 hardcoded [], no producer)"  # 4ee8
    status: todo_soft  # 4ee8
    priority: 3  # 4ee8
    parent:  # 4ee8
    tags:  # 4ee8
    - schema  # 4ee8
    - docs  # 4ee8
    - dead_field  # 4ee8
    - self_analysis  # 4ee8
    - dogfooding  # 4ee8
    isbefore: []  # 4ee8
    duplicate_of: []  # 4ee8
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 4ee8
    pr_ref:  # 4ee8
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 12).**\n\n**Symptom.** The behavior-map JSON's top-level `features[]` field is always empty. On hypergumbo's self-analysis:\n\n```json\n\"features\": []\n```\n\nThe producer is `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/schema.py:101`:\n\n```python\ndef create_empty_behavior_map() -> dict:\n    return {\n        ...\n        \"features\": [],\n        ...\n    }\n```\n\nAnd no code anywhere appends to it.\n\n**Why this is a problem.** The spec documents `features[]` as a first-class top-level field with full schema:\n\n`docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:798-802`:\n\n> ### features[] — named slices\n>\n> Each feature contains `id`, `name`, `entry_nodes[]`, `node_ids[]`, `edge_ids[]`, a `query` object (method, entrypoint, hops, max_files, exclude_tests), `limits_hit[]`, and `summary`. See `docs/schema.json` for the full structure.\n>\n> **Feature ID:** Stable identifier based on query spec: `sha256(json.dumps(query, sort_keys=True))`. Same query on same code → same feature ID → enables diff across commits.\n\n`docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:1701` also notes:\n\n> `features[]`: Array of objects, each with `id`, `name`, `entry_nodes[]`\n\n`docs/schema.json:99` declares the JSON-Schema entry for `features`.\n\nSo spec/schema commit to a feature, but the producer commits nothing. Slice output IS produced by `hypergumbo slice` (we observed it in Round 10's exploration) — but slices go to separate files (e.g., `slice.entry_name.json`) with `view: \"slice\"`, not into `behavior_map.features`.\n\n**Severity.** Low-medium. Consumers reading the spec see a documented schema and write code expecting it to populate (e.g., \"diff features across commits\"). On every real behavior map they get an empty array. Same pattern as WI-fanun (fingerprint declared, mostly null).\n\n**Fix proposal — two viable approaches.**\n\n1. **Populate features[] on `hypergumbo run`.** Run the default set of feature-extractions (e.g., one per entrypoint) and embed their slices into `behavior_map.features`. Existing slice machinery is reusable. Larger scope.\n\n2. **Document the gap and remove the field.** Update spec + schema + producer to drop `features[]` entirely. If users want feature/slice output, they invoke `hypergumbo slice` explicitly. Smaller change; closes the documentation-rot loop.\n\nRecommended: **2 for now, 1 as future work.** Drop the empty field from the schema and remove the spec section, OR rewrite the section to say \"Features are produced by `hypergumbo slice` and stored in separate slice JSON files; the behavior_map does not embed features by default.\"\n\n**Test gap.** Add a schema-conformance test that asserts `behavior_map[\"features\"] == []` (codifying current behavior) AND a doc-test that fails if a `## features[]` section exists in `hypergumbo-spec.md` claiming it's populated.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/schema.py:87-105` — empty initializer\n- `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:798-802` — the spec section\n- `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:1701` — array-typed declaration\n- `docs/schema.json:99` — JSON-Schema declaration\n- `WI-fanun-...` (filed round 9) — same pattern (`fingerprint` field declared but mostly null)\n- `WI-luvig-...` (filed round 7) — same pattern (`canonical_name` declared but null on mainstream-analyzer source)"  # 4ee8
    fields: {}  # 4ee8
- op: update  # eeda
  at: '2026-05-17T21:42:20Z'  # eeda
  by: agent  # eeda
  actor: jgstern_agent  # eeda
  clock: 2  # eeda
  nonce: eeda  # eeda
  set:  # eeda
    description: "**REVISED 2026-05-17 — original kill-the-field framing was wrong.** Investigation confirmed that `features[]` is documented in the spec as a stable public contract, not vestigial schema rot. The 100% empty state means implementation diverged from spec, not that the field is dead.\n\n## Empirical observation (unchanged)\n\nThe behavior-map JSON's top-level `features[]` field is always empty on hypergumbo's self-analysis:\n\n```json\n\"features\": []\n```\n\nThe producer is `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/schema.py:101`:\n\n```python\ndef create_empty_behavior_map() -> dict:\n    return {\n        ...\n        \"features\": [],\n        ...\n    }\n```\n\nNo code anywhere appends to it.\n\n## What the spec actually says\n\n`features[]` is not a stub. The spec at `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:798-802` documents it as a first-class public surface with full schema and stable IDs:\n\n> ### features[] — named slices\n> Each feature contains `id`, `name`, `entry_nodes[]`, `node_ids[]`, `edge_ids[]`, a `query` object (method, entrypoint, hops, max_files, exclude_tests), `limits_hit[]`, and `summary`. See `docs/schema.json` for the full structure.\n> **Feature ID:** Stable identifier based on query spec: `sha256(json.dumps(query, sort_keys=True))`. Same query on same code → same feature ID → enables diff across commits.\n\nThe spec also makes consumer compatibility commitments. At line 1717: *\"Consumers MUST ignore unknown fields in `nodes[]`, `edges[]`, `features[]`, `analysis_runs[]`.\"* That treats `features[]` as part of the public versioned contract.\n\n`docs/schema.json:99` declares the JSON-Schema entry.\n\n## The actual gap: spec-implementation divergence\n\n`hypergumbo slice` writes feature outputs to **separate files** (`slice.<entry>.json` etc.) with `view: \"slice\"`, not into the behavior map's `features[]`. So the spec's \"named slices, queryable by stable ID, diffable across commits\" contract is real on paper but unrealized in code.\n\n**100% empty is empirical evidence of divergence, not evidence the field is dead.** The original kill-the-field framing skipped the design call.\n\n## Severity (refined)\n\nLow-medium. A consumer reading the spec sees a documented schema and writes code expecting it to populate (e.g., \"diff features across commits\"). On every real behavior map they get an empty array — broken contract.\n\n## Two real dispositions (it's a design call, not a default)\n\n### (a) Implementation catches up to spec\n\n`hypergumbo slice` writes both to separate files (current behavior, for explicit slice invocations) AND embeds slices into `behavior_map.features` for the default analysis run. The slice-machinery already exists and is reusable. The stable-ID-on-query design at spec line 802 becomes operative for diff-across-commits use cases.\n\nScope:\n- Pick a default-set of features to compute on every `hypergumbo run` (e.g., one feature per entrypoint, or per `usage_context`).\n- Wire slice machinery into the behavior-map builder.\n- Stable-ID derivation matches the spec formula (`sha256(json.dumps(query, sort_keys=True))`).\n\n### (b) Spec catches up to implementation\n\nUpdate `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md` to retire the `features[]` section (lines 798-802) AND the line 1701 / 1717 references. Document `slice.<entry>.json` files as the canonical surface for feature output. Drop `features[]` from `docs/schema.json:99` and from `schema.py:101`.\n\nThe MUST-ignore clause loses one element; consumers that relied on `features[]` (if any exist) must migrate to reading slice files. Spec + impl stay consistent post-change.\n\n**Neither (a) nor (b) is \"drop without retiring spec.\"** The original recommendation conflated \"the field is empty\" with \"the design intent doesn't exist\" — they're different. Empty is a producer state; the design intent is in the spec.\n\n## Related\n\nOther fields/contracts in similar \"declared but not realized\" state — see `WI-lonoz` (`Edge.quality` 🟪 design placeholder), `INV-tofur` (`AnalysisRun.repo_fingerprint` spec-defined algorithm not implemented), `WI-fanun` / `INV-sotiv` / `WI-luvig` (now parented under META `INV-zadum` write-side identity sub-META). This item is not under any META; consider whether a \"spec-vs-implementation reconciliation\" framing would benefit from one. For now, leave as a standalone design call.\n\n## Test gap\n\nOnce a disposition is chosen, add a property test pinning the chosen behavior:\n- (a): assert `len(behavior_map[\"features\"]) > 0` on a fixture with entrypoints\n- (b): assert `\"features\"` is not present in behavior-map keys after the spec retirement lands"  # eeda
